<section class="panel" aria-label="Subtract Strategically source-based Grade 3 lesson">
<h2>Warm-up — Number Talk: Threes</h2>
<p>Find these multiplication products mentally, then explain how two groups plus one more gives three groups.</p><ol><li>2 × 6</li><li>3 × 6</li><li>2 × 7</li><li>3 × 7</li></ol>
<h2>Activity 1 — How Would You Subtract?</h2>
<p>For each difference, choose your own valid algorithm, number line or compensation. Show work to a partner, and discuss why that method suits these numbers.</p><ol><li>451 − 329</li><li>382 − 190</li><li>924 − 285</li><li>600 − 478</li><li>505 − 417</li></ol>
<div style="border:1px solid #7d93a8;border-radius:8px;padding:12px"><strong>Two valid 600 − 478 representations</strong><p>Count on: 478 → 480 (+2) → 600 (+120). Difference 122.</p><p>Algorithm: exchange hundreds and tens as necessary so the ones, tens and hundreds can be subtracted correctly; also yields 122.</p></div>
<h2>Activity 2 — Greatest Difference, Smallest Difference</h2>
<p>In pairs, choose whether to aim for a greatest or smallest difference. Use the <strong>original 2021 spinner supplied by your teacher</strong>; place successive spun digits as hundreds, tens or ones in two three-digit numbers. Subtract, compare partners' outcomes using &gt;, &lt; or =, and play another round.</p>
